  "policy_title": "Lift the VAT threshold to \u00a3150,000",
  "outcome": "O2",
  "verdict": {
    "outcome": "O2",
    "direction": "mixed",
    "magnitude": "minor",
    "time_horizon": "this-parliament",
    "confidence": "moderate",
    "plain": "Raising the VAT threshold to \u00a3150,000 could let some small businesses charge lower prices, helping consumers \u2014 but it costs over \u00a32bn in public revenue and mainly benefits business owners rather than shoppers directly. The net effect on ordinary people's cost of living is small and uncertain.",
    "biggest_unknown": "Whether businesses freed from VAT actually pass savings on to consumers as lower prices, or simply pocket the margin.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy proposes lifting the VAT threshold to \u00a3150,000 to free small businesses from red tape.",
        "evidence_id": "M",
        "quote": "Reform UK will lift the VAT threshold to \u00a3150,000 to free up small entrepreneurs from red tape."
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "The current UK VAT registration threshold is \u00a390,000 as of April 2024.",
        "evidence_id": "E2",
        "quote": "As of April 2024, the UK's VAT registration threshold is \u00a390,000, having been increased from \u00a385,000."
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Businesses below the VAT threshold can potentially offer lower prices to consumers compared to VAT-registered competitors.",
        "evidence_id": "E13",
        "quote": "Businesses below the VAT threshold do not charge VAT on their sales, allowing them to potentially offer lower prices to non-VAT registered customers (e.g., consumers) compared to their VAT-registered competitors."
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "Reform UK estimates the policy would cost more than \u00a32bn in forgone tax revenue.",
        "evidence_id": "E11",
        "quote": "Reform UK estimates this \"tax break\" would cost \"more than \u00a32bn\""
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "A higher threshold risks shifting the 'bunching' distortion to a new, higher level rather than eliminating it.",
        "evidence_id": "E10",
        "quote": "critics argue it could simply shift the \"bunching\" problem to the new, higher threshold, creating a larger distortion there."
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "Raising the threshold could help around 320,000 firms avoid VAT registration.",
        "evidence_id": "E7",
        "quote": "aligning with Reform UK's claim of helping 320,000 firms."
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "The policy's direct relevance to cost of living is indirect: it does not change tax thresholds or benefits for households, but it may marginally reduce prices for goods and services sold by newly exempted small businesses, since those firms no longer need to charge VAT. This is a real but limited channel \u2014 the price effect depends entirely on businesses passing savings through to consumers rather than retaining them as profit margin. On the downside, a \u00a32bn-plus revenue cost could, depending on other fiscal choices, put pressure on public services or require other tax rises that squeeze household budgets. The bunching distortion is also projected to shift upward rather than disappear, limiting efficiency gains. The IFS and CIOT both caution against narrowing the VAT base further. The net cost-of-living effect for ordinary households is therefore modest at best: some may benefit from marginally lower prices at small local businesses, but the fiscal cost is large relative to the consumer benefit, and there is no direct income support or energy/food cost mechanism. The verdict is mixed but minor \u2014 a small potential consumer price benefit offset by a large fiscal cost with uncertain pass-through.",
